---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../../components/PageHeader.astro';
import ScoreCard from '../../components/ScoreCard.astro';
import MetaScoreBadge from '../../components/MetaScoreBadge.astro';
import VerdictBox from '../../components/VerdictBox.astro';
import CTA from '../../components/CTA.astro';
import DisclosureBlock from '../../components/DisclosureBlock.astro';
import LastVerified from '../../components/LastVerified.astro';
import FAQ from '../../components/FAQ.astro';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';
import { SITE } from '../../data/site';

const app = appBySlug('yousician');
const title = 'Is Yousician Worth It in 2026? Cost vs. Value for Piano Learners';
const description =
  'Is Yousician worth it for piano? We break down the real cost ($20/mo or $140/yr), the multi-instrument bundle math, and where piano-first apps beat it. MetaScore 7.9/10.';
---

<BaseLayout title={`${title} | ${SITE.name}`} description={description}>
  <PageHeader title={title} crumbs={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'Is Yousician worth it?' }]}>
    Short answer: as a piano app alone, no — there are better piano-first options for the same money.
    As a <em>multi-instrument subscription</em>, it can be a genuine bargain. Here is the math for both cases,
    based on our data (MetaScore <MetaScoreBadge app={app} showSources={false} />).
    <ScoreCard app={app} linkReview slot="aside" />
  </PageHeader>
  <div class="container">
    <div class="prose">
    <h2>What Yousician costs in 2026</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>List price:</strong> $20/month or $140/year — one subscription covers piano, guitar, ukulele, bass, and singing.</li>
      <li><strong>Trial:</strong> 7-day free trial, card required.</li>
      <li><strong>Context:</strong> the annual plan is $11.67/month per month-equivalent — cheaper than most piano-only apps. But you are splitting that value across instruments you may never touch.</li>
      <li><strong>Honesty note:</strong> Yousician's subscription billing draws the most negative reviews of any app we track — watch for auto-renewal and cancellation friction. Set a calendar reminder before the trial converts.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>What you get for it</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>One subscription for five instruments — unbeatable if you actually play more than one.</li>
      <li>The strongest gamification in the category: missions, streaks, and leaderboards keep kids (and competitive adults) practicing.</li>
      <li>The widest platform support of any app here: iOS, Android, PC, and Mac.</li>
      <li>A 10,000-song catalog — though it spans all instruments, not just piano.</li>
      <li>Zero-setup mic input; no cables or configuration.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>What you do not get</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Piano depth. As a piano-only product, Yousician is a generalist: the piano curriculum is shallower than <a href="/flowkey-review/">Flowkey</a> ($120/yr, MetaScore 8.9) or <a href="/simply-piano-review/">Simply Piano</a> ($150/yr, MetaScore 8.6).</li>
      <li>Sheet music. Yousician has no real sheet-music focus — a dealbreaker if you want to play classical repertoire or read notation.</li>
      <li>MIDI support. Feedback is mic-only, which struggles in noisy rooms.</li>
      <li>Intermediate depth. Intermediates hit a ceiling; <a href="/pianote-review/">Pianote</a> is the honest upgrade path for serious learners.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>The bundle math: when Yousician beats piano-only apps</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>You play piano + guitar (or ukulele/bass):</strong> Yousician at $140/yr replaces two subscriptions. Flowkey + a comparable guitar app would run you roughly $200–280/yr combined. Clear win.</li>
      <li><strong>You only want piano:</strong> Flowkey gives you a better-rated piano experience (MetaScore 8.9 vs 7.9) for $20 less per year. Simply Piano's beginner curriculum is more structured. There is no reason to pay for instruments you will not use.</li>
      <li><strong>Kids learning piano (only):</strong> this is Yousician's closest piano-only case — the gamification genuinely engages kids. But <a href="/simply-piano-review/">Simply Piano</a>'s kids profiles are purpose-built for piano and cost only $10 more per year.</li>
    </ul>
    <VerdictBox title="The worth-it test">
      Yousician is worth it if you learn more than one instrument — at $140/yr for five, the bundle math is
      the best deal in music apps, and it is the only plan here that covers piano, guitar, ukulele, bass, and
      singing. It is not worth it as a piano-only subscription: Flowkey beats it on ratings and price,
      and Simply Piano beats it on curriculum structure. Buy the bundle, not the piano course.
    </VerdictBox>
    <CTA app={app} label="Try Yousician free for 7 days" />
    <p>Still deciding? <a href="/yousician-review/">Read our full Yousician review</a>, <a href="/flowkey-vs-yousician/">compare it head-to-head with Flowkey</a>, or <a href="/best-piano-apps/">see the full 2026 ranking</a>.</p>
    <FAQ items={[
      { q: 'How much does Yousician cost per month?', a: '$20/month at list price, or $140/year on the annual plan — one subscription for piano, guitar, ukulele, bass, and singing. There is a 7-day free trial (card required).' },
      { q: 'Is Yousician good for learning piano?', a: 'It is decent for beginners, but it is a generalist: the piano curriculum is shallower than piano-first apps like Flowkey or Simply Piano, and there is no sheet-music focus. Buy it for the instrument bundle, not for the piano course.' },
      { q: 'Is Yousician cheaper than Simply Piano?', a: 'Yes on paper: $140/yr vs Simply Piano\u2019s $150/yr. But Flowkey ($120/yr, MetaScore 8.9) undercuts Yousician while rating higher for piano specifically.' },
      { q: 'Can you cancel Yousician easily?', a: 'Subscription and cancellation complaints are the most common negative Yousician reviews. Cancel through your platform\u2019s subscription settings (App Store, Google Play) rather than hunting for a button in the app, and do it before the trial converts.' },
    ]} />
    <DisclosureBlock />
    <LastVerified />
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
